% warrants.bib — the substrate worklist as a claim-DAG.
%
% THE GOVERNING INVARIANT: every claim here is UNDISCHARGED until its check
% passes, and the item is incomplete until it can be discharged.  Those are the
% same fact — a claim whose check exits non-zero is a claim the gate refuses to
% certify, so `open item` and `red check` are one state, computed fresh on every
% run.
%
% ⚑ THERE IS NO open/closed FIELD, AND THERE MUST NEVER BE ONE.  Openness is
% computed from exit codes; nothing records it, so nothing about it can go
% stale.  Recording open-ness is precisely the failure mode this design exists
% to dissolve.
%
% `claim` is the COMMITMENT — one line.  The finding it came from lives in
% plan.md and is cited by section number.  Do not grow these into paragraphs:
% the one-line limit is what forced T17's figure into a checkable [tag].
%
% This file is SOURCE and is meant to be edited.  WORKLIST.md is the projection
% and must never be hand-edited — the gate refuses it.

% ── tooling ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
% ⚑ `enables` IS SEQUENCING, NOT GROUNDING, and the two must not be conflated.
% `rests-on` says a claim cannot be TRUE unless its premises are; `enables` says
% the work is cheaper or safer in this order.  T17 does not make A5 true -- it
% makes A5's gate askable without an env-var bypass, which is what "tooling
% first" means when it is measured rather than asserted.  The ordering counts
% `enables` toward LEVERAGE and never toward the topological layer, so a tool
% can rank first without becoming a false premise (plan sections 21, 65).
@misc{T17,
  section = {tooling},
  enables = {A5, R8, G1, G6, R2-SWEEP, R9-residue, S1, T12-rederive},
  claim   = {--dry-run is reachable on every ratchet gate, so a gate that mints or lowers a baseline can be asked what it would do without doing it [gates-without-dryrun=0]},
  check   = {item:T17}
}

