% ⚑ A VERIFY DEADLINE UNDER THE STORE'S OWN LATENCY FLOOR IS A FALSE NEGATIVE,
% AND WE SHIPPED ONE.  `scripts/membudget_otlp.py:147` polls read-your-write for
% 30s.  VictoriaMetrics' `-search.latencyOffset` DEFAULTS TO 30s -- an instant
% query evaluates at `now - offset` -- so on a stock store the datapoint is not
% queryable until the deadline has already expired.  The verify reports
% `NOT queryable` on data that ingested correctly.
%
% ⚑ THE FIGURE IS PAPERKIT'S MEASUREMENT, NOT MY INFERENCE: 30.77s at the
% default vs 10.5s at `-search.latencyOffset=1s`.  Two independent producers
% pushing to one store is what made the constant visible; substrate's own arc
% never ran long enough against a stock configuration to see it.
%
% ⚑ AND IT IS THE `vm_rows_inserted_total` CLASS ONE LAYER DOWN.  That episode
% (substrate found it, cassian corrected the guide, paperkit was warned before
% implementing) was a verify predicate that reported failure on success.  This
% is the same defect in the REPLACEMENT predicate -- read-your-write is correct
% and its deadline is not, so the fix that retired the counter can still fail.
% A verify that cries wolf is worse than none: it trains the operator to
% disbelieve the one instrument that distinguishes ingested from dropped.
%
% NOT part of OBS, deliberately.  OBS is the series item and this is a defect in
% a file that ships today, true whether or not OBS ever lands -- but it
% `enables` OBS, because OBS's emit path IS this producer and would inherit the
% deadline unchanged.  ⚑ It also dissolves what I filed as a precondition: I
% wrote that OBS must first prove the push path end-to-end, since the store
% currently holds ZERO `membudget_*` series.  Read-your-write IS that proof --
% a value the producer controls, queried back, is unaffected by whether the
% producer stopped or the store was reset.  There is no separate liveness step,
% only a deadline long enough for the answer to arrive.
@misc{OBS-DEADLINE,
  section = {tooling}, from = {OBS}, join = {. },
  enables = {OBS},
  claim   = {every read-your-write verify allows for the store's `-search.latencyOffset`, so a correct push is never reported as a failed one [verify-deadline-under-floor=0]},
  check   = {item:OBS-DEADLINE}
}

