% ⚑ THE ENGINE IS PARTIALLY ADOPTED AND I KEPT BUILDING ALONGSIDE IT.  Surveyed
% 2026-08-02 (findings stashed at `catalog/library/paperkit-architecture.md`, with
% witnesses): `worklist_gate.py:196` ALREADY IMPORTS `paperkit.bib` -- so this is
% not "should we adopt it", it is an adoption that stopped at the parse and
% re-rolled everything above it.  That is the harder case: the seam exists, and
% every reuse-search I ran this session looked PAST it because the import was
% already there and read as done.
%
% ⚑ AND THE SHARPEST DUPLICATION IS ONE I DEFENDED ALL SESSION.  `order()`'s
% comments insist `enables` (sequencing) is distinct from `rests-on` (grounding);
% paperkit has no `enables` -- it has `from` (prose order) vs `rests-on`
% (grounding), which is EXACTLY that distinction (`bib.py:28-30`: *"the two are
% often reversed"*).  I reinvented the second edge under a third name, and
% `bib._LIST` whitelists `from`/`rests-on`/`reads`, so the engine DROPS `enables`
% on every read.  Every ordering decision this session ran on an edge the engine
% cannot see.
%
% Filed as THREE routes because the remedies differ -- a rename, a delegation, and
% a semantics change are not one item (the A5 and T25 correction, third time).
%
% ⚑ AND THE FIRST ROUTE'S CLAIM WAS WRONG, CAUGHT BY CHECKING ONE ENTRY BEFORE
% TOUCHING FOURTEEN.  I filed this as "respell `enables` as `from`", reading the
% agent's finding as a rename.  Then `--source dep_order` (`bib.py:96`) showed
% `from` means *visit my antecedents first* -- so `B from A` orders A before B,
% which is what `A enables B` says from the other end.  A direction flip, not a
% rename.  Fine so far.
%
% ⚑ EXCEPT `OBS-DEADLINE` CARRIES BOTH `from = {OBS}` AND `enables = {OBS}`.
% Under the engine that pair is a CYCLE; under our reading it is coherent and
% says two different things: *cite OBS in the prose* and *do OBS-DEADLINE first*.
% So the two fields are NOT one relation spelled twice -- `from` is CITATION
% order, `enables` is WORK order, and they genuinely diverge on at least one
% entry.  Respelling would have asserted a citation nobody intended and put a
% self-cycle in the graph, on 14 entries, mechanically.
%
% The honest claim is therefore NOT "respell".  It is that the work-order edge
% must be legible to the engine WITHOUT being conflated with citation order --
% which is the foreign-reference route (a derived bib the engine imports,
% carrying `check = concept:<key>`), not a field rename.  Re-sized accordingly;
% the 14 entries are not a mechanical sweep and must not be treated as one.
@misc{T28-edges,
  section = {tooling}, from = {T27}, join = {. },
  claim   = {the work-order edge is legible to the engine without being conflated with citation order --- `enables` is not `from`, and one entry carries both toward the same key [engine-dropped-edges=0]},
  check   = {item:T28-edges}
}

