@misc{grandfathered,
  section  = {decide},
  claim    = {This claim exists to be rested on by a retraction whose real justification cannot yet be synthesized, so the grounding is NAMED and ENUMERATED rather than left empty -- and the set of claims resting on it may not grow},
  check    = {item:U1},
  note     = {kind=decision by=momentum, on the operator's design. WHY A CLAIM AND NOT A SECTION, KIND OR FIELD (operator, verbatim): it can then be referenced by the existing worklist claims where premises are structurally mandatory, but where we do not have enough information to synthesize the reason for the retraction. So the exemption is an ENTRY like any other, and taking it is an ordinary rests-on edge -- which is what makes the population READABLE. THE RATCHET ENUMERATES ITS INBOUND EDGES, IT DOES NOT COUNT THEM (operator: not counting, enumerating). A count over this set is exactly the defect the repo records elsewhere -- six names versus six names with DIFFERENT SETS -- so one claim could leave and another arrive while the number held level and the ratchet reported nothing. The gate must name WHICH claims rest here, and refuse a key that was not in the baseline. THE RATCHET IS SELF-DISSOLVING, which is the whole design: to rest on this you must write why, and once written it IS a premise, so the escape route costs exactly what the correct route costs and produces the correct artefact. The only way through the hatch is the way you would have gone anyway -- stronger than forbidding it, because a forbidden hatch gets routed around and this one cannot be. A SELF-GROUNDED RETRACTION IS VACUOUS BUT NOT FALSE (operator), so this converts an unmarked vacancy into a named, enumerated, non-growing population rather than treating it as an error. THE CHECK IS BORROWED DELIBERATELY: item:U1 is the decision witness, which discriminates on whether a pending decision is LEGIBLE rather than whether it is made, and a grandfathered claim IS a legible pending decision. It gets its own witness when the enumerating gate lands.}
}

